          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"verona-colei-che-non-si-deve-amare-6e05cf6014ac655b\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"The highlighted “Ti amo” is principally a direct avowal that presents the speaker’s love as true. Although it occurs within an intense erotic solicitation, it does not itself clearly undertake a commitment. The slow, sustained, and rhetorically coherent speech provides no positive evidence that these particular words are a reflex-like expressive discharge rather than an emotionally charged assertion.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The surrounding declarations of surrender (“Sono tua”) and requests for intimacy give the avowal relational and persuasive force, but that context does not clearly make the core “Ti amo” itself performative.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"The woman directly says “Ti amo” to Rigo during an intimate face-to-face appeal.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal is embedded in a prolonged attempt to secure kisses, physical closeness, and a sexual encounter. Her languor, agitation, and desire make it emotionally intense, while the sequence’s coherent appeals and imperatives also make it strategically seductive. Neither intensity nor persuasive use displaces its ordinary force as a claim of love. The later identification of Rigo as her brother frames the encounter as forbidden and conflicted, but that narrative circumstance concerns the avowal’s context rather than requiring a category beyond T/P/E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Ti amo, e sola non potrò dormire... perchè ho voglia che tu mi baci.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit declaration presents her loving state as true and connects it to her desire for his physical affection, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She begins speaking softly, “facendo lunghe pause, con trepidazione,” and develops an extended sequence of reasons, requests, and invitations.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal belongs to sustained, intelligible discourse rather than being represented as an involuntary blurt or reflex-like verbal discharge; this weighs against E.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately around the avowal she says “Báciami!”, “Sono tua... tua... préndimi!” and asks to come into his bed.\",\"supports\":\"The declaration operates within an erotic solicitation and has persuasive relational force, but the explicit surrender and proposed action are expressed by adjacent utterances rather than undertaken by “Ti amo” itself.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After the encounter the narration calls Rigo “il fratello.”\",\"supports\":\"The sibling relationship explains the forbidden, conflicted narrative framing without altering the core avowal’s T/P/E classification.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative Italian present-form explicit love expression, exact match “Ti amo.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the target occurrence and its unhedged affirmative grammatical form.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Its erotic intensity, persuasive purpose, and forbidden relational context can be described contextually without treating them as independent core forces or invoking O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
